<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  Overhead ("bird's-eye") camera for Gazebo Classic + ROS 1.

  Publishes  /birdseye/image_raw            (sensor_msgs/Image)
             /birdseye/image_raw/compressed (sensor_msgs/CompressedImage)
             /birdseye/camera_info

  The point of this model is that the bird's-eye view becomes a ROS TOPIC with
  header stamps on the same simulation clock as the robot's own camera. Both
  can then go into one bag and be merged with no guessing about offsets, and
  the result is correct however badly the real-time factor wanders.

  POSE.  The model is placed by the spawn command (-x -y -z -Y), NOT here.
  The <pose> below is the sensor's orientation INSIDE the model, and pitch
  +1.5708 is what aims the lens straight down:

      pitch +1.5708  ->  view direction (0, 0, -1)   straight down    CORRECT
      pitch -1.5708  ->  view direction (0, 0, +1)   straight up      WRONG

  Gazebo renders along the sensor's +X axis. Do NOT add the ROS optical-frame
  rotation (-1.5708 0 -1.5708) here: that only belongs on a frame_id / TF
  frame, never on the sensor pose.

  Spawning with -Y <yaw> rotates the overhead view. The top of the image points
  along world direction (cos yaw, sin yaw):

      -Y 0        top of image = world +X
      -Y 1.5708   top of image = world +Y
      -Y 3.1416   top of image = world -X
      -Y -1.5708  top of image = world -Y

  HEIGHT AND FIELD OF VIEW.  Ground coverage is
      width  = 2 * h * tan(horizontal_fov / 2)      h = height ABOVE THE FLOOR
      height = width * image_height / image_width

  With the 60 deg (1.0472 rad) default below:
      h =  8 m ->  9.2 x 5.2 m        h = 13 m -> 15.0 x 8.4 m
      h = 10 m -> 11.5 x 6.5 m        h = 16 m -> 18.5 x 10.4 m

  Go HIGH with a NARROW lens. Tall walls near a low, wide-angle camera show up
  as big slanted faces and the image reads like a front view even though the
  lens points straight down. The university_corridor world has 3 m walls and
  no ceiling, so spawn well above the walls (z >= 10). Never spawn below the
  wall tops or inside a wall's plane.

  If a world DOES have a roof, a camera above it sees only the roof: stay
  below the ceiling and widen the FOV, cut a hole, or use an oblique view.

  UPDATE RATE.  Every rendered frame costs simulation speed, and raw frames are
  enormous to record: 1280x720 bgr8 at 30 Hz is 83 MB/s, about 5 GB per minute.
  15 Hz at 960x540 is plenty for a presentation and is a quarter of the cost.
  Record the /compressed topic (see record_run.sh) and it drops by roughly 40x.
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <model name="birdseye">
    <!-- Static: never falls, never collides, does not perturb the robot. -->
    <static>true</static>

    <!-- No <visual> and no <collision> on purpose: the camera must be
         invisible in its own view and must not obstruct the robot. -->
    <link name="birdseye_link">
      <sensor name="birdseye_cam" type="camera">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 1.5708 0</pose>
        <update_rate>15</update_rate>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <visualize>false</visualize>

        <camera>
          <!-- 60 deg. Other values: 45=0.7854, 80=1.3963, 100=1.7453 -->
          <horizontal_fov>1.0472</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>960</width>
            <height>540</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <!-- far must exceed the camera height or the floor is clipped away -->
            <near>0.1</near>
            <far>100</far>
          </clip>
        </camera>

        <plugin name="birdseye_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_camera.so">
          <alwaysOn>true</alwaysOn>
          <!-- 0.0 means "use the sensor's own update_rate above" -->
          <updateRate>0.0</updateRate>
          <cameraName>birdseye</cameraName>
          <imageTopicName>image_raw</imageTopicName>
          <cameraInfoTopicName>camera_info</cameraInfoTopicName>
          <frameName>birdseye_link</frameName>
        </plugin>
      </sensor>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
